{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md | sed -n '10,125p' && nl -ba collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md | sed -n '125,215p' && sed -n '375,415p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"    10\t### 1.1 Why round 3 failed, and the one change that fixes the class\n    11\t\n    12\tThe engine's reference refcount counts **hashtable instances**. Rounds 2 and 3 tried to count holders over persistent values, which have no instance identity, so every copy/remove/separate case fell between the rules. The fix is to give an array an instance identity **exactly when the engine's bookkeeping needs one**, and never otherwise:\n    13\t\n    14\t- A **plain array** is a persistent ordered-map value, stored directly in its holder (the §8.11 rule). Invariant: **a plain array contains no reference marker and no instance handle, transitively.** Copying it is O(1) and needs no counting because there is nothing inside it to count.\n    15\t- An **instance array** is a heap cell `{:php/type :arr :rc n :value <ordered-map> :deps #{keys}}`: `:rc` is the engine's hashtable refcount, `:deps` the set of keys whose slots hold a marker or a handle (small; usually empty). A holder stores the handle `{:php/arr h}`.\n    16\t- A **reference** is a heap cell `{:php/type :ref :holders n :value x}`; a marker `{:php/ref c}` sits in a variable cell, a property slot or an **instance** slot. `:holders` is the engine's `zend_reference` refcount exactly: one per variable/property holder plus one per instance slot holding the marker.\n    17\t- **Instantiation**: an array becomes an instance the moment a marker or a handle is installed into one of its slots. Installing at a path converts every plain array on the path to an instance (rc 1 each, the parent slot takes the handle, `:deps` updated). So every container between a variable and a marker is an instance, and every copy of one is a counted operation.\n    18\t- **Separation** is explicit and happens exactly where the engine duplicates: a write through a handle whose `:rc > 1`. Its rule is `zend_array_dup`'s.\n    19\t- **Unwrapping** happens only at separation (a holders-1 marker is unwrapped *in the copy*; the source keeps it). There is no \"demotion at write\": an in-place write through a holders-1 marker writes into the cell, as the engine does, which is observably identical.\n    20\t- **Scope exit** is a counted release under the profile `:yang.php/exact-refcount true` (default on): the lowering wraps every function body whose scope declares a local in `php/try-finally` with `php/release-locals`, run on normal return and on every escape. Off, scope exit releases nothing and the departure D-scope below applies.\n    21\t\n    22\tThe one *unavoidable* departure: an object that dies without `unset` releases nothing (no finalizers), so handles and markers held only by a dead object's properties stay counted (D-object). An unneeded separation caused by an over-count is **unobservable**: with the marker's holders at 1 the unwrapped copy and an in-place write read the same; with holders above 1 the copy keeps the marker and writes through, as the in-place write would. Over-counting therefore costs allocation, never semantics. Under-counting is impossible because every decrement corresponds to an explicit acquire.\n    23\t\n    24\t### 1.2 Contents and operations\n    25\t\n    26\tContent kinds a location can hold: `S` scalar/string/null/object handle, `P` plain array, `I1` handle to an instance with `rc = 1`, `In` handle with `rc > 1`, `R1` marker to a cell with `holders = 1`, `Rn` marker with `holders > 1`. A location is a variable cell, an object property slot, or an instance slot. \"Release\" and \"Separate\" are the two internal procedures:\n    27\t\n    28\t```text\n    29\tRelease(content):   S, P -> nothing (P is marker- and handle-free by invariant)\n    30\t                    I(h) -> rc(h) -= 1; if 0 then Destroy(h)\n    31\t                    R(c) -> holders(c) -= 1; if 0 then Release(value(c)); c is garbage\n    32\tDestroy(h):         for k in deps(h): Release(slot k of h); h is garbage\n    33\tSeparate(h) [rc>1]: h' := new instance {rc 1, value(h), deps(h)}; rc(h) -= 1\n    34\t                    for k in deps(h), slot content x:\n    35\t                      I(g)              -> rc(g) += 1                         (dup addrefs nested ht)\n    36\t                      R(c), holders>1   -> holders(c) += 1                    (both hashtables hold it)\n    37\t                      R(c), holders=1, value(c) is handle to h itself\n    38\t                                        -> holders(c) += 1                    (self-reference exception)\n    39\t                      R(c), holders=1   -> slot k of h' := value(c) as a copy  (unwrap in the copy:\n    40\t                                           P shares; I(g) rc(g)+=1; S copies); deps(h') drops k\n    41\t                                           unless the value is a handle\n    42\t                    the writer's location now holds h'\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\t### 1.3 The transition table\n    46\t\n    47\tEach row: the operation, then its effect for each content kind of the location it reads from or writes to. \"walk\" means: at each path step, a marker is dereferenced into its cell; a handle with `rc > 1` is separated and the parent location rebound to `h'`; a plain array is updated persistently.\n    48\t\n    49\t| # | Operation | S | P | I1 | In | R1 | Rn |\n    50\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    51\t| 1 | Copy `$y = $x` (also by-value param bind, return, array-literal element, `foreach` by-value snapshot) | copy | share O(1) | rc+1 → In | rc+1 | deref, then the row of value(c) | same |\n    52\t| 2 | Bind `$y =& $x` where `$x` holds | new cell c{holders 2, value}; both locations ← marker | same (P moves into c; no instantiation of `$x`'s own container needed unless `$x` is a slot: then instantiate the path to it) | same, value is the handle | same | `$y` ← same marker; holders 2 | holders+1 |\n    53\t| 3 | Bind into a slot `$y =& $a[k]` / `[&$x]` / `foreach as &$v` / by-ref arg `f($a[k])` | instantiate path to `$a[k]` (plain → I1 at each level, handles installed, deps updated); then row 2 on the slot; `deps(h) += k` | | | | | |\n    54\t| 4 | Write `$x = v` (variable or property, not through `&`) | Release(old); store | same | same (rc−1 of old) | same | write **through**: value(c) := v (holders unchanged) | same |\n    55\t| 5 | Path write `$x[k…] = v` | TypeError/auto-vivify per PHP | persistent update | walk; in place | **Separate** then in place | at terminal slot: write through | same |\n    56\t| 6 | Store into a slot, terminal content kinds | S/P: set; if slot held I/R: Release(old), deps−k | storing a handle: rc+1, deps+k | | | | |\n    57\t| 7 | `unset($x[k])` | — | remove | walk (separate if rc>1), remove; Release(slot content); deps−k | same after Separate | slot R1: holders→0, Release(value) | holders−1 |\n    58\t| 8 | `unset($x)` (variable/property) | unbind | unbind | Release → rc−1 (Destroy at 0) | rc−1 | holders−1 → 0, Release(value) | holders−1 |\n    59\t| 9 | Rebind `$x =& other` | Release(old) then row 2 | same | same | same | holders−1 then row 2 | same |\n    60\t| 10 | Read `$x`, `$x[k…]` | value | value | value(h) (no count) | same | deref (no count) | same |\n    61\t| 11 | Scope exit (exact-refcount on) | — | — | Release every local: row 8 per local | | | |\n    62\t| 12 | Scope exit (off) | nothing (departure D-scope) | | | | | |\n    63\t| 13 | `foreach ($a as &$v)` | per iteration: Release(`$v`'s old marker) then row 3 on slot i; after the loop `$v` keeps the last marker | | | | | |\n    64\t| 14 | `global $x` / `static $x` | row 2 against the `$GLOBALS` entry / the function's static cell (install creates it as null if absent) | | | | | |\n    65\t| 15 | Builtins consuming arrays | read (row 10); results are plain arrays (departure D-builtins: functions the engine documents as preserving references, such as `array_values` over a shared slot, return marker-free copies) | | | | | |\n    66\t| 16 | `$a[0] =& $a` | row 3 instantiates `$a` (I1) and installs; `$a`'s own variable becomes a marker holder too; at separation the self-reference exception keeps it | | | | | |\n    67\t\n    68\tEvery count change in the table is an explicit lowering operation; no copy acquires a holder it does not count, and no removal releases one that was not acquired. The reviewer's three programs:\n    69\t\n    70\t| Program | Engine | Table |\n    71\t|---|---|---|\n    72\t| `$a=[1]; $r=&$a[0]; unset($r); $b=$a; $b[0]=2; echo $a[0];` | 1 | row 3: `$a` I1, c{2}; row 8: c{1}; row 1: rc 2; row 5: Separate → unwrap (holders 1) into `$b`'s copy, write; `$a[0]` reads c, **1** |\n    73\t| `$a=[1]; $r=&$a[0]; $b=$a; unset($r); $b[0]=2; echo $a[0];` | 1 | c{2}, rc 2, c{1}, Separate → unwrap, **1** |\n    74\t| `$a=[1]; $r=&$a[0]; $b=$a; unset($b[0]); $a[0]=2; echo $r;` | 2 | c{2}, rc 2; row 7: Separate `$b` (holders>1 → c{3}), remove → c{2}; row 5 on `$a` (rc 1) in place, through c → `$r` **2** |\n    75\t\n    76\t### 1.4 Fixture set (P3), `ref-lifecycle-v1`\n    77\t\n    78\t- **One fixture per non-empty cell of the table** (rows 1 to 16 against each applicable content kind), each a PHP program whose expected output is produced by the pinned PHP 8.3 CLI; the generator script and the fixture file are checked in (the `c3-corpus-v1` pattern).\n    79\t- **The permutation corpus**: over the operation alphabet `{C: $b=$a, U: unset($r), W: $b[0]=2, X: unset($b[0]), A: $a[0]=3, D: unset($b), N: $c=$b; $c[1]=9}` starting from `$a=[1]; $r=&$a[0];`, every sequence of length 2 to 5 (duplicates allowed), printing `$a`, `$b`, `$c`, `$r` with `var_dump`, oracle from the engine. This is the \"every order\" requirement made mechanical: roughly 20,000 programs, generated and compared in the slow tier; a sample of 200 in the quick tier.\n    80\t- **Nested paths**: the same alphabet with `$a['x'][0]` and `$r =& $a['x'][0]`, exercising instantiation along the path and nested separation.\n    81\t- **Self reference**: `$a[0]=&$a` then copy and write.\n    82\t- **Scope exit**: a function that separates a copy holding a live reference and returns without `unset`, under both profile values; the exact profile matches the engine, the other is the D-scope fixture.\n    83\t- **Objects**: a property holding an instance array and a marker, with `unset($obj->p)` (counted) and object death (D-object fixture).\n    84\t- **Builtins**: `array_values`, `array_merge`, `array_slice` over an array with a shared slot (D-builtins fixtures).\n    85\t- **Renderer**: `var_dump` prints `&` for a slot whose marker has `holders > 1` only (the engine shows the reference flag only for shared references; verified by the oracle, not assumed).\n    86\t\n    87\tOwner decision **B8.8 (revised)**: instance arrays on demand with exact engine bookkeeping (recommended), `:exact-refcount` default on. Departures: D-object, D-builtins, and D-scope only when the profile is off. Cost: one cell per array that ever receives a reference, O(|deps|) per separation, one `try-finally` frame per call of a function with locals.\n    88\t\n    89\t---\n    90\t\n    91\t## 2. ES modules: the complete operation matrix\n    92\t\n    93\t### 2.1 Phases and the job rule\n    94\t\n    95\tThree phases over module records (cells in `js.rt/modules`, status `:unlinked | :linking | :linked | :evaluating | :committed | :failed`, plus `:error`, `:dfs-index`, `:dfs-ancestor`):\n    96\t\n    97\t- **Link** (declare): deliver the static graph from the root, create records, **allocate every environment cell of every module in the graph**, run every `init` (hoisted functions), resolve import bindings to the exporters' cells, build namespace objects, detect missing or ambiguous exports. Link is idempotent: a record already `:linked` or beyond is skipped; a link failure leaves every record it created `:unlinked` again (no partial environments survive). Only `:unlinked` records are touched.\n    98\t- **Evaluate** (commit per component): the DFS of round 3 with an explicit stack; members commit together when their root pops; a throw fails every record on the stack from the thrower upward, all with the same error object, and propagates.\n    99\t- **Settle** (dynamic only): a promise is resolved with a namespace or rejected with an error, from the module's **final** status.\n   100\t\n   101\t**The job rule** (P1-2): a dynamic `import(spec)` performs no linking or evaluation synchronously. It creates a promise and enqueues a **job** on the prelude's job queue; the job runs only when no evaluation is in progress, which is guaranteed because synchronous evaluation cannot suspend (top-level `await` is refused in Phase 1) and the job queue is drained by the scheduler between jobs, never inside a body. Round 3's synchronous `:evaluating` shortcut is withdrawn. The job then runs Link and Evaluate from `spec`'s record as a fresh top-level evaluation (new DFS stack) and settles from the record's status. Two consequences fall out: a module that calls `import(\"./self\")` and then throws `e` has status `:failed e` when the job runs, so the promise rejects with **that `e`**; a module that imports itself and succeeds resolves to its own namespace after its component committed.\n   102\t\n   103\t### 2.2 The matrix\n   104\t\n   105\tRows are the operation; columns the status of the target record **at the moment the operation reaches it**.\n   106\t\n   107\t| Operation | `:unlinked` | `:linking` | `:linked` | `:evaluating` | `:committed` | `:failed` |\n   108\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   109\t| Static import, link phase | deliver, create, allocate env, run `init`, resolve (recursively) | cycle in the static graph: use the record's env as is (already allocated); no recursion | skip | skip | skip | skip |\n   110\t| Static import, evaluate phase (from an evaluating importer) | impossible (link completed first) | impossible | recurse: push, index, run; ancestor = min | **cycle**: importer's ancestor := min(importer, target); no recursion; proceed | continue | rethrow `:error` (fails the importer's component) |\n   111\t| Body throws | — | — | — | every record on the stack from the thrower upward → `:failed` with this error; propagate | — | — |\n   112\t| Body returns, this is the SCC root | — | — | — | pop to and including self; all popped → `:committed` | — | — |\n   113\t| Body returns, not the root | — | — | — | stay `:evaluating` on the stack | — | — |\n   114\t| `import(spec)` expression (synchronous part) | create promise, enqueue job | same | same | same | same | same |\n   115\t| `import()` job runs | Link from this record (may add records; a link error rejects the promise, records restored to `:unlinked`), then Evaluate as a new top-level DFS, then settle | impossible (no link in progress between jobs) | Evaluate as a new top-level DFS; settle | **impossible by the job rule**; a defect if observed (asserted) | resolve with the namespace | reject with `:error` |\n   116\t| Two `import()` jobs for one module | first job links and evaluates; the second sees `:committed`/`:failed` and settles accordingly; each has its own promise | | | | | |\n   117\t| `import()` of a member of a component that failed elsewhere | reject with the component's error (the same object every member carries) | | | | | |\n   118\t| Static import of a module previously evaluated by a dynamic job | link skips it; evaluate sees `:committed` or `:failed` | | | | | |\n   119\t| Dynamic import of a module whose static graph includes already-committed modules | link skips them; the new DFS treats them as `:committed` and does not re-run bodies | | | | | |\n   120\t| REPL `(reset)` | all records dropped with the task (one task per process, as Python) | | | | | |\n   121\t\n   122\t**What is committed when.** Environment cells and hoisted functions: at link, before any body (so a cyclic call into a hoisted export works). Namespace objects: at link (reading env cells live). Status `:committed`: only when the component root pops. Status `:failed`: for every unfinished member on the stack above the thrower, at the throw. Promises: at the end of the dynamic job, from final status, never earlier.\n   123\t\n   124\t**Why the dynamic path composes with the static rules.** Both run the same `link-module` and `evaluate-module` over the same records; the dynamic path differs only in its entry (a job after any synchronous evaluation has finished) and its exit (settlement from final status). Because link only touches `:unlinked` records and evaluate only recurses into `:linked` ones, a dynamic graph that overlaps a static one re-does nothing, and because no evaluation is in progress when a job runs, a dynamic import can never observe `:evaluating`. Publication SCC bundles are invisible to both: a member's record is an ordinary record whose code lives in a bundle.\n   125\t\n   125\t\n   126\t### 2.3 Fixtures (T7)\n   127\t\n   128\tCycle commit and fail (round 3's set), plus: `import(\"./self\")` then throw: the promise rejects with the identical error object, in the same microtask order Node gives; `import(\"./self\")` then success: resolves to the namespace after commit, `.then` runs after the module's synchronous body completes; two `import()` of one module resolve to the same namespace object with the body run once; `import()` of a member of a failed component rejects with the shared error; `import()` whose link fails (missing export in a newly delivered module) rejects with the link error and leaves no partial record (a later correct import links afresh); dynamic import of a module whose graph contains committed modules does not re-run them (side-effect counter); an `import()` expression evaluated inside a body does not run before the body returns (ordering fixture against Node); top-level `await` refusal diagnostic.\n   129\t\n   130\t---\n   131\t\n   132\t## 3. Claims: the evidence-replay rule\n   133\t\n   134\tThe observer (typecheck.md §2.2 item 3, §7) indexes a packet's claims from its `claims` cursor up to `claims-t`, emits the verdict, frees the index and advances. A late `.d.ts` claim arrives after that cursor has moved on, so \"bind on the next packet\" (round 3) gave the observer no way back to the original claims. Rule:\n   135\t\n   136\t- **What evidence.** Every claim row on the `claims` medium whose occurrence root is the packet's owning root, lying in the position interval `[from, t]`, where `from` is the position of the first claim ever appended about that root and `t` the watermark of the recheck. Both original and late claims are in that interval; nothing is reconstructed from memory or from a prior verdict.\n   137\t- **Who records `from`.** The publisher, at first publication: `[<manifest-address> :yang.claims/from <position>]` beside the manifest and the binder table (§A10). A root with no claims has no `from`, and no recheck is ever requested for it.\n   138\t- **Who requests a recheck, and when.** A composition-level **recheck requester** (not the frontend, not the observer): it appends a packet to `typecheck-in` that is **byte-identical** to the published tree packet (the observer's \"forwarded bytes equal input bytes\" law holds trivially) with an envelope carrying `:yang.claims/from` and `:yang.claims/t` (the current `claims` position). It is triggered by a late declaration publication (the `.d.ts` compile appends its claims, then the requester appends the recheck) or on demand.\n   139\t- **How the observer replays.** On a packet carrying `:yang.claims/from`, the observer opens a **fresh cursor** on `claims` at that position (an explicit `stream/cursor` anchor; the medium's retention is a declared policy, §6.4), reads to `t`, indexes only rows for this root, evaluates the checker, emits the verdict citing exactly the `claim-refs` it used, frees the index, and discards the fresh cursor. Its ordinary cursor is untouched, so the per-packet bound of §2.2 item 3 and every §7 prohibition hold (no prior verdict, no `dao.space.index`, no unbounded buffer). A `gap` on the fresh cursor (retention lost) yields `:refused` with rule `:evidence-unavailable`: an honest verdict, never a silent accept.\n   140\t- **Topology.** In `gate`, the recheck packet is forwarded or withheld like any other; evaluators that already hold the earlier forwarded copy are unaffected (a verdict cannot un-run a program; the recheck's refusal is a fact a composition reads). In `report`, the new verdict simply supersedes by time. This is the typecheck design's own \"verdicts cite their evidence\" (decision 10) applied to time.\n   141\t\n   142\t**T10 sequence fixture** (exactly the reviewer's order): publish module with `export let x: number` (claims appended at `from`), first packet → `:accepted` citing one ref; compile a `.d.ts` against the module claiming `x: string` (late claims appended); recheck packet with `[from, t]` → `:refused` (binder declared twice with unequal types) citing **both** refs; a second recheck after retention is cut below `from` → `:refused :evidence-unavailable`.\n   143\t\n   144\t---\n   145\t\n   146\t## 4. T10 arity fixture rescoped (P2)\n   147\t\n   148\tEvery guest call lowers to `js/call f this args`, so no guest binder is the operator of a UAST application; the T1 claims-consistency checker's arity rule has no meeting point to see, and a guest-aware analysis would be a checker beyond its contract. Ruling: T10's **frontend acceptance** uses the conflicting-declaration fixtures (§3 above and round 3's `x: number` versus `x: string`), which need only binder claims and binder tables. The **generic arity rule** is exercised by a hand-built UAST fixture, a direct application `(f 1 2)` with an arity claim on `f`'s binder, in `typecheck_contract_test`'s territory, proving observer plumbing, not TS. Guest-aware meeting points (`js/call` as a call site, PHP's `php/call` likewise) are listed as the first requirement of a future checker slice and are out of both frontends' scope.\n   149\t\n   150\t---\n   151\t\n   152\t## 5. Updated slices and owner decisions\n   153\t\n   154\t| Slice | Change |\n   155\t|---|---|\n   156\t| P3 | the §1 model; `ref-lifecycle-v1` fixture file and generator; the permutation corpus (slow tier); `:exact-refcount` profile and its `try-finally` wrap |\n   157\t| T7 | the job rule; the §2 matrix fixtures; the ordering fixtures against Node |\n   158\t| T10 | `:yang.claims/from` published beside the manifest; the recheck requester as a test composition; the four-step sequence fixture; the arity fixture moved to the contract test |\n   159\t| L3 | the lifted publisher records `:yang.claims/from` when a frontend supplies claims (Python supplies none) |\n   160\t\n   161\tOwner decisions: **B8.8** revised as above (instance-on-demand arrays, exact profile default on). **A12.13** new: dynamic import strictly as a job, top-level `await` refused in Phase 1 (recommended) versus implementing asynchronous module evaluation in T7. **A12.14** new: recheck by evidence replay from the `claims` medium with a declared retention (recommended) versus a persistent claims index inside the observer (forbidden by typecheck.md §7) versus no rechecks (late `.d.ts` would then be useless).\n   162\t\n   163\t---\n   164\t\n   165\t## 6. What round 4's reviewer should probe\n   166\t\n   167\t1. **The invariant \"plain arrays are marker- and handle-free, transitively\"**: whether every operation that could place a handle or marker into a plain array goes through instantiation (array literals with `&`, `array_walk` by reference, `extract(EXTR_REFS)`, `list()` with `&`, `compact` of a reference variable), and whether row 6 (storing a handle into a slot) can leave a plain array holding a handle (it converts the parent at store time; the table should be read that way, and the reviewer may want it as an explicit row).\n   168\t2. **Separation cost**: whether `:deps` stays small in realistic programs (an array with thousands of reference slots separates in O(thousands)); this is the engine's cost too, but a reviewer may want it measured in P3.\n   169\t3. **The permutation corpus size** and whether `var_dump`'s `&` flag is the right oracle for holder counts (it shows the flag only for shared references; a holders-1 marker prints plain).\n   170\t4. **The job rule's dependence on \"no top-level await\"**: whether refusing TLA is acceptable for Phase 1 and whether any other construct can suspend a synchronous module evaluation (generators and `await` inside functions cannot; a host effect park inside a body can, on this VM, which does not exist in the engine: a parked body is still \"evaluating\" and a job must not run meanwhile, so the scheduler must drain jobs only when the evaluation stack is empty. I expect this to be probed and it is stated here as the rule).\n   171\t5. **Evidence replay retention**: who sets the `claims` retention and what the composition does when `:evidence-unavailable` appears in `gate`; and whether a fresh cursor on an append-only medium at an arbitrary past position is supported by every `dao.stream` backing medium (file and memory media support oldest-anchored cursors today; a position anchor is a `dao.stream` question the slice must confirm).\n   172\t6. **The hand-built arity fixture** being outside the TS frontend: whether that is an honest scoping of T10 or a gap the reviewer wants listed in the known-gaps ledger (I recommend the ledger row \"guest-aware call meeting points for the checker\", owner seat: typecheck).\n;; The `cell` module\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A cell is a mutable location in the task's `:heap`, reached by a sealed\n;; `:cell-ref` (Architect cell ruling, slice 1). These are pure effect\n;; constructors like the stream module's: they touch no heap, and the engine\n;; interprets the effects they return. The heap is VM state, so a mutation\n;; is shared by every closure over the ref and is never rolled back when a\n;; continuation is invoked (box semantics).\n\n(defn new-cell\n  [v]\n  (make-effect :cell/new {:val v}))\n\n\n(defn get-cell\n  [c]\n  (make-effect :cell/get {:cell c}))\n\n\n(defn set-cell!\n  [c v]\n  (make-effect :cell/set! {:cell c, :val v}))\n\n\n(def cell-module\n  \"The `cell` module definition. Registering it is a composition step.\"\n  {'new new-cell, 'get get-cell, 'set! set-cell!})\n\n\n(def cell-profiles\n  \"UCF 7.5.2 profiles for the `cell` module's bindings: each an\n   `:effectful` pure effect constructor declaring its one effect kind.\"\n  {'new (vm/primitive-profile 'new :effectful [1] #{:cell/new} :none)\n   'get (vm/primitive-profile 'get :effectful [1] #{:cell/get} :none)\n   'set! (vm/primitive-profile 'set! :effectful [2] #{:cell/set!} :none)})\n\n\n(defn register-cell-module\n  \"Return registry with the `cell` module registered.\"\n  [registry]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
