{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-17 08:00:19 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-17 15:00:19 +0700\n\nReviewed the complete revision against HEAD `5f8963093631ab447840cd6a041d83fadccf3999`; tracked files match HEAD. No files edited. Verification used committed source, Zend source and in-memory Node probes. PHP examples below were checked against Zend’s operations; PHP CLI was not run.\n\n1. **[P1] Reference binding into an existing shared array omits separation.**  \n   [Table row 3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:53) instantiates the path and binds the slot, but does not invoke the separating `walk`:\n   ```php\n   $a = [1, 2]; $r =& $a[0];\n   $b = $a;\n   $s =& $b[1]; $s = 9;\n   echo $a[1]; // PHP: 2\n   ```\n   `$a` and `$b` already share an instance. Installing the second marker without separation modifies their shared table, producing `9`. Zend separates the array when obtaining its writable element address. [Zend implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.c).\n\n   Define reference binding, by-reference argument access and by-reference iteration through the writable-path operation. Add binding-after-copy fixtures; the proposed permutation alphabet contains no new reference binding.\n\n2. **[P1] Rebinding releases the destination before protecting an aliased source.**  \n   [Table row 9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:59) specifies `Release(old) then row 2`. When source and destination are the same sole reference holder, this destroys the source before reuse:\n   ```php\n   $a = [1]; $r =& $a[0];\n   $z =& $a; unset($z);\n   $a =& $a; // must be a no-op\n   ```\n   The ruled release drops the outer reference to zero and destroys its array. Source-within-destination cases require protection too. Zend explicitly handles identical locations and acquires the source reference before releasing the destination. [Zend reference assignment](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.c).\n\n   Specify alias-safe acquisition/release ordering, including identical locations and sources inside the old destination.\n\n3. **[P1] Assignment through a reference omits release of its previous value.**  \n   [Table row 4](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:54) changes `value(c)` without releasing the old array handle:\n   ```php\n   $a = [1]; $r =& $a[0];\n   $x =& $a;\n   $a = 0;\n   $b = [&$r]; unset($r);\n   $c = $b; $c[0] = 2;\n   echo $b[0]; // PHP: 1\n   ```\n   Under the table, the abandoned array retains its slot’s reference holder. Separation consequently preserves sharing and produces `2`. Neither D-object nor D-builtins applies. Zend assignment dereferences the box and destroys the replaced referent while retaining the reference itself. [Zend value assignment](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.h).\n\n   Define replacement of reference contents with acquisition of the new value and release of the old value. This also disproves the general claim that over-counting affects allocation only.\n\n4. **[P1] Evidence replay requires an unsupported position anchor.**  \n   [Replay rule:139](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:139) calls `stream/cursor` with a saved position. The governing [cursor contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:503) guarantees only `oldest` and `newest`; it expressly prohibits fabricated positions. Both [memory-log](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:103) and ringbuffer reject other anchors.\n\n   Save an actual stream-minted immutable cursor before the first relevant append, then replay from that cursor. This also preserves eviction detection. Alternatively, declare and implement a transport-specific anchor dependency. Leaving this for the slice to “confirm” does not establish an implementable replay contract.\n\n5. **[P1] Initially unannotated modules cannot acquire late checking evidence.**  \n   [Replay rule:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:137) records `from` only at first publication and forbids rechecks for roots initially lacking claims. That excludes ordinary JavaScript and default-erased modules subsequently supplied with declaration files.\n\n   Define initialization when the **first claim** arrives, even after code publication. Test an initially claim-free module, a late `.d.ts`, and a second conflicting `.d.ts`; the recheck must cite both declarations.\n\n6. **[P2] A single import job does not establish the promised Node microtask ordering.**  \n   The cycle-completion and self-import error rules are now sound. However, the [ordering acceptance](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792217000000-architect-ts-php-r4.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:127) promises Node parity beyond what the single-job rule specifies.\n\n   With an already-loaded module, Node v26.1.0 produced `t1,t2,t3,t4,import` against a four-step promise chain. Enqueuing one job that immediately resolves the import promise produced `t1,import,t2,t3,t4`. Pin the reference runtime and define the required settlement scheduling, or qualify this ordering claim.\n\nThe original unset and copy/remove fixtures now pass under the ruled transitions. SCC commitment, deferred dynamic-import evaluation and the T10 arity rescoping address their earlier findings. The PHP ownership table and evidence-replay contract still require changes before sign-off.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES"}}
